#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046668} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566295} :activity/id #uuid "b45be7aa-3389-5d04-be7f-6c12e62bd7a0" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566295 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360483 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07d395ff-c9b8-57ab-ace3-d37966cc5235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2t{e}^{-st}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357822} {:answer/id #uuid "146dd4ab-8bac-5175-8723-3891d22237e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2t{e}^{st}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357927} {:answer/id #uuid "4962113f-d78c-5fe7-ae12-fd3b934aa764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358381} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd19d0f-60a8-5109-bf7a-caf1ec7c5da7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t({e}^{st}-{e}^{-st})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358589} {:answer/id #uuid "a83c3833-66a8-57a8-8cf8-f30d37c589b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t({e}^{2st}+{e}^{-2st})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359204}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358589} :answer-field/id #uuid "64926fc3-40d3-52da-bc14-bf84d37251ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2df77054-e089-51e6-a47e-2d85379912a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-94747" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{y},$ where $x={e}^{st}$ and $y={e}^{-st}.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\frac{1}{({e}^{st})^{2}}=-{e}^{-2st}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\frac{1}{({e}^{-st})^{2}}=-{e}^{2st}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =t{e}^{st}, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =-t{e}^{-st}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(-{e}^{-2st})(t{e}^{st})+(-{e}^{2st})(-t{e}^{-st}) \\\\  & =t{e}^{st}-t{e}^{-st} \\\\  & =t({e}^{st}-{e}^{-st}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6473ded1-3ff1-56db-b8af-a5723874b273" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566318}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566304 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199007 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bf91dd3-a758-5a4f-91d7-508b8452bd7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197917} {:answer/id #uuid "4cfe915e-292f-5afc-a987-feaf8645491b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480883} {:answer/id #uuid "50bbbf00-20cb-560c-a0ab-ee8b2c1c6005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480887} {:answer/id #uuid "69ed5ffb-e837-50a7-83fa-9b0d9378cfad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}-e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480936} {:answer/id #uuid "a5e7b49a-7376-5f77-b4cd-07e49c94ad93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e-2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481051}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480883} :answer-field/id #uuid "64ff9f93-9496-5f90-8fb6-64a192e2c4d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f7817742-b76a-5484-9fbf-d87d76ee15f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-82916" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x+2y){e}^{(x+2y)^{2}}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 5,\\,0\\le x+2y\\le 1\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 5,\\,0\\le x+2y\\le 1,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x+2y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 5,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+2y){e}^{(x+2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}v{e}^{{v}^{2}}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}v{e}^{{v}^{2}}\\,|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{5}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}v{e}^{{v}^{2}}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{5}{[\\frac{{e}^{{v}^{2}}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{5}(e-1)\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(e-1)[u]_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(e-1)(5-1) \\\\  & =e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a60b997e-1b94-5fa6-9acb-4e89ec821663" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566306}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566306 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447158 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26a04d3a-0123-57f0-b050-a4cee7844a9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{3}\\,\\theta \\sqrt{1+9{cos}^{2}\\,\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447096} {:answer/id #uuid "3795af39-24b8-5073-90c3-faa50ef95682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}\\,\\theta \\sqrt{1+8{cos}^{2}\\,\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447103} {:answer/id #uuid "4d4c2c76-9822-5adf-bfb2-5991320c3105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }sin\\,\\theta \\sqrt{1+6{cos}^{2}\\,\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447108} {:answer/id #uuid "8c59bfa2-430e-5926-995c-ce492668dd20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }sin\\,\\theta \\sqrt{1-8{sin}^{2}\\,\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447119} {:answer/id #uuid "9d63f17c-0ef7-5244-bb03-4939eb982145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}\\,\\theta \\sqrt{4+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447124}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447103} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce35695c-d7ed-5650-b6f0-c0d7e8432851" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7aa4e4d5-0080-5cb6-bebe-9d6c94950127" :question/math-academy-id "q-123511" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi $ ?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3{sin}^{2}\\,\\theta \\text{ }\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{({sin}^{3}\\,\\theta )}^{2}+{(3{sin}^{2}\\,\\theta \\text{ }\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{sin}^{6}\\,\\theta +9{sin}^{4}\\,\\theta \\text{ }\\,{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}\\,\\theta \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}\\,\\theta +9{cos}^{2}\\,\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}\\,\\theta \\sqrt{1+8{cos}^{2}\\,\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "abc6980e-2066-5e2f-89bf-6eb7df162872"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566318 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566273 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d9be715-36a9-5f87-aaff-b73f3c392482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565726} {:answer/id #uuid "64fb7564-0e54-5edc-bf06-22fc601176e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565835} {:answer/id #uuid "7625faaf-7a06-5f8d-9f7a-5f31afa9fee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565862} {:answer/id #uuid "82e0bdf4-a937-5979-9022-49f3096a8334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565884} {:answer/id #uuid "d5496e58-5442-54c8-8ae7-8fd4a6dda1d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566022}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565884} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb17ec4b-63ee-5206-af27-3498015a3fd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f7c6f3ba-6533-5a1f-b464-492440ca8f6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-163179" :question/problem "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}$\nand has mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=y,$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters.\nGiven that the center of mass is at $(1,2),$ find the moment of inertia about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\text{m}$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, we find the mass of the plate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =x{|}_{0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass $(1,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}((x-\\overset{¯}{x})^{2}+(y-\\overset{¯}{y})^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}((x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}((x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2})y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(x-1)^{2}y+{y}^{3}-4{y}^{2}+4y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}-\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3}+2{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{9}{2}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}(x-1)^{3}+\\frac{9}{4}x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{9}{2}-(-\\frac{3}{2}+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\text{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =12\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f1dde27c-6d1b-5b88-844c-736e84bed360" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566304}}] :activity/title "Quiz 5" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045460 :db/ident :activity.type/assessment} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565671}]}
